# ================= C =================
X("c", "c_pointers", "easy", "Can you explain pointers in C with a tiny example? I keep mixing up * and &.",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 10;\n    int *p = &x;      /* p holds the address of x */\n    *p = 25;          /* write through the pointer */\n    printf("%d %d\\n", x, *p);\n    printf("%d\\n", p == &x);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "`&x` means \"the address of `x`\". `int *p` declares a pointer that stores an address. `*p` (dereference) means \"the thing `p` points to\". Because `p` points at `x`, assigning to `*p` changes `x`. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nThe second line shows that `p` really equals the address of `x`.", "25 25\n1")
X("c", "c_pointers", "medium", "Why doesn't my swap function work in C?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid swap_wrong(int a, int b) { int t = a; a = b; b = t; }\nvoid swap_right(int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 1, y = 2;\n    swap_wrong(x, y);\n    printf("%d %d\\n", x, y);\n    swap_right(&x, &y);\n    printf("%d %d\\n", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "C passes arguments **by value**: `swap_wrong` receives copies, so swapping them doesn't affect the caller. To modify the caller's variables, pass their addresses and write through the pointers. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```", "1 2\n2 1")
X("c", "c_pointers", "medium", "How do arrays and pointers relate in C?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n    int *p = a;                       /* array decays to pointer to first element */\n    printf("%d %d %d\\n", a[2], *(p + 2), p[2]);\n    printf("%zu %zu\\n", sizeof a, sizeof p == sizeof(void *));\n    return 0;\n}',
  "In most expressions an array name decays to a pointer to its first element, so `a[2]`, `*(p + 2)` and `p[2]` all mean the same thing, and pointer arithmetic moves in units of the element type. But an array is not a pointer: `sizeof a` is the size of the whole array (16 bytes with 4-byte ints), whereas `sizeof p` is the pointer size. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\n(The second number prints 1 because I compare `sizeof p` with the size of a generic pointer.)")
X("c", "c_memory", "medium", "How do I allocate and free memory properly in C?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = 5;\n    int *arr = malloc(n * sizeof *arr);\n    if (!arr) { perror("malloc"); return 1; }\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = i * i;\n    long sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += arr[i];\n    printf("%ld\\n", sum);\n    free(arr);\n    arr = NULL;\n    return 0;\n}',
  "Pattern: `malloc(count * sizeof *ptr)` (using `sizeof *ptr` keeps the size right if the type changes), check for `NULL`, use the memory, then `free` it exactly once. Setting the pointer to `NULL` afterwards prevents accidental use-after-free from looking valid. This prints `{out}`. Every `malloc` needs a matching `free`; tools such as Valgrind or AddressSanitizer find leaks and invalid accesses.", "30")
X("c", "c_memory", "hard", "What's wrong with returning a pointer to a local variable?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nchar *make_greeting(const char *name) {\n    char *buf = malloc(strlen(name) + 8);\n    if (!buf) return NULL;\n    strcpy(buf, "Hello, ");\n    strcat(buf, name);\n    return buf;      /* heap memory outlives the function */\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    char *g = make_greeting("Ana");\n    printf("%s\\n", g);\n    free(g);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "A local array like `char buf[32]` lives on the stack and is destroyed when the function returns, so a pointer to it becomes dangling (undefined behaviour). To return data, allocate it on the heap, as above, and document that the **caller must free it**, or let the caller pass in a buffer and size. Output: `{out}`.", "Hello, Ana")
X("c", "c_memory", "hard", "What does a buffer overflow look like and how do I avoid it in C?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    char dst[8];\n    const char *src = "this string is far too long";\n    /* strcpy(dst, src);   would write past the end of dst */\n    snprintf(dst, sizeof dst, "%s", src);   /* bounded and always NUL-terminated */\n    printf("%s (%zu chars kept)\\n", dst, strlen(dst));\n    return 0;\n}',
  "`strcpy` copies until it finds a NUL byte and does not know the destination size, so a long source overwrites adjacent memory, which is a classic security flaw. Use bounded functions: `snprintf(dst, sizeof dst, ...)` truncates safely and terminates the string. Output: `{out}`. Always pass the real buffer size and prefer `sizeof dst` over a hard-coded number. `strncpy` is easy to misuse because it may not NUL-terminate.", "this st (7 chars kept)")
X("c", "c_struct", "medium", "Show me how to use structs and function pointers together in C.",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n\ntypedef struct {\n    const char *name;\n    int (*op)(int, int);\n} Operation;\n\nstatic int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nstatic int mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    Operation ops[] = {{"add", add}, {"mul", mul}};\n    for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++)\n        printf("%s(6, 7) = %d\\n", ops[i].name, ops[i].op(6, 7));\n    return 0;\n}',
  "A function pointer stores the address of a function with a given signature, so you can choose behaviour at runtime, which is C's basic form of polymorphism (callbacks, dispatch tables, `qsort` comparators). A struct bundles the name with the function. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```", "add(6, 7) = 13\nmul(6, 7) = 42")
X("c", "c_struct", "medium", "How does qsort work with a custom comparison function?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\ntypedef struct { const char *name; int age; } Person;\n\nstatic int by_age(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    const Person *x = a, *y = b;\n    return (x->age > y->age) - (x->age < y->age);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Person p[] = {{"Ana", 31}, {"Bo", 22}, {"Cy", 27}};\n    qsort(p, 3, sizeof p[0], by_age);\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) printf("%s ", p[i].name);\n    printf("\\n");\n    return 0;\n}',
  "`qsort` sorts any array: you give the base pointer, element count, element size and a comparator returning negative, zero or positive. The comparator receives `const void *` and casts back to the element type. Writing `(a > b) - (a < b)` avoids the overflow that `a - b` can cause. Output: `{out}` (youngest first).", "Bo Cy Ana")
X("c", "c_struct", "hard", "How do I implement a simple stack in C?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\ntypedef struct {\n    int *data;\n    size_t size, cap;\n} Stack;\n\nstatic int push(Stack *s, int v) {\n    if (s->size == s->cap) {\n        size_t ncap = s->cap ? s->cap * 2 : 2;\n        int *nd = realloc(s->data, ncap * sizeof *nd);\n        if (!nd) return 0;\n        s->data = nd; s->cap = ncap;\n    }\n    s->data[s->size++] = v;\n    return 1;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Stack s = {0};\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) push(&s, i * 10);\n    while (s.size) printf("%d ", s.data[--s.size]);\n    printf("\\n");\n    free(s.data);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "A growable stack is a dynamic array plus a size. When full, double the capacity with `realloc` (always assign to a temporary first, so you don't lose the old pointer if it fails). Doubling gives amortised O(1) pushes. Output: `{out}` (last in, first out). Remember to `free` the buffer.", "50 40 30 20 10")
X("c", "c_strings", "easy", "How do C strings work? Why does my comparison with == fail?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    char a[] = "hello";\n    char b[] = "hello";\n    printf("%d\\n", a == b);            /* compares addresses */\n    printf("%d\\n", strcmp(a, b) == 0); /* compares contents */\n    printf("%zu %zu\\n", strlen(a), sizeof a);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "A C string is an array of `char` ending with a NUL byte (`\\0`). `==` on two arrays compares their addresses, which differ, so it gives false. Use `strcmp`, which returns 0 when the contents are equal. Note `strlen` counts characters before the NUL (5), while `sizeof` of the array includes it (6). Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```")
X("c", "c_bits", "medium", "How do bit flags work in C?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n\nenum { READ = 1 << 0, WRITE = 1 << 1, EXEC = 1 << 2 };\n\nint main(void) {\n    unsigned perms = READ | WRITE;\n    perms |= EXEC;          /* set */\n    perms &= ~WRITE;        /* clear */\n    printf("%u %d %d\\n", perms, (perms & READ) != 0, (perms & WRITE) != 0);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "Give each flag its own bit. `|` sets bits, `& ~FLAG` clears them, and `& FLAG` tests them. Here the value starts as READ|WRITE (3), gains EXEC (7) and loses WRITE (5). Output: `{out}`: permissions 5, read is set, write is not.", "5 1 0")
X("c", "c_memory", "hard", "What is undefined behaviour with signed overflow? Show a safe check.",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <limits.h>\n\nstatic int safe_add(int a, int b, int *out) {\n    if ((b > 0 && a > INT_MAX - b) || (b < 0 && a < INT_MIN - b)) return 0;\n    *out = a + b;\n    return 1;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int r;\n    printf("%d\\n", safe_add(INT_MAX, 1, &r));\n    int ok = safe_add(2, 3, &r);\n    printf("%d %d\\n", ok, r);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "In C, signed integer overflow is undefined behaviour: the compiler may assume it never happens, so a check done *after* the addition can be optimised away. Test before you add, using the limits. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nUnsigned overflow, by contrast, is well-defined and wraps around.")

# ================= C++ =================
X("cpp", "cpp_raii", "medium", "What is RAII in C++ and why is it better than calling cleanup manually?",
  '#include <iostream>\n\nstruct Guard {\n    const char *name;\n    explicit Guard(const char *n) : name(n) { std::cout << "acquire " << name << "\\n"; }\n    ~Guard() { std::cout << "release " << name << "\\n"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    try {\n        Guard a("file");\n        Guard b("lock");\n        throw 1;\n    } catch (int) {\n        std::cout << "caught\\n";\n    }\n}',
  "RAII (resource acquisition is initialisation) ties a resource's lifetime to an object: the constructor acquires, the destructor releases. Destructors run automatically when the scope ends, **even when an exception unwinds the stack**, and in reverse order of construction. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nThis is why you rarely write `delete` or `close()` by hand in modern C++.")
X("cpp", "cpp_smart", "medium", "unique_ptr vs shared_ptr - when do I use which?",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <memory>\n\nstruct Widget { ~Widget() { std::cout << "destroyed\\n"; } };\n\nint main() {\n    auto u = std::make_unique<Widget>();\n    std::shared_ptr<Widget> s1 = std::make_shared<Widget>();\n    {\n        auto s2 = s1;\n        std::cout << "count " << s1.use_count() << "\\n";\n    }\n    std::cout << "count " << s1.use_count() << "\\n";\n}',
  "Use `unique_ptr` by default: exactly one owner, no overhead, and the object is destroyed when it goes out of scope. Use `shared_ptr` only when ownership is genuinely shared, since it carries a reference count. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nThe count rose to 2 while `s2` existed and fell back to 1. Both widgets are destroyed at the end of `main`. Prefer `make_unique` and `make_shared` over raw `new`.")
X("cpp", "cpp_move", "hard", "Explain move semantics with something I can run.",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <utility>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string a = "a fairly long string that lives on the heap";\n    std::string b = std::move(a);      // steals the buffer\n    std::cout << "b=" << (b.size() > 0) << " a_empty=" << a.empty() << "\\n";\n\n    std::vector<int> v(1000, 7);\n    std::vector<int> w = std::move(v);\n    std::cout << w.size() << " " << v.size() << "\\n";\n}',
  "`std::move` doesn't move anything by itself: it casts to an rvalue reference so the move constructor can **steal** the internal buffer instead of copying it. The source is left valid but unspecified (in practice empty). Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nMoving a vector is O(1) (swap three pointers) while copying it is O(n). Don't use a moved-from object except to assign to it or destroy it.")
X("cpp", "cpp_stl", "medium", "Which STL container should I pick: vector, list, map or unordered_map?",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <map>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> v = {5, 1, 4};\n    v.push_back(9);\n    std::map<std::string, int> ordered = {{"pear", 3}, {"apple", 5}, {"fig", 1}};\n    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> hashed(ordered.begin(), ordered.end());\n    for (auto &kv : ordered) std::cout << kv.first << " ";\n    std::cout << "\\n" << v.size() << " " << hashed.at("fig") << "\\n";\n}',
  "Default to `std::vector`: contiguous memory, cache-friendly and fast. Use `std::map` (balanced tree, O(log n)) when you need sorted iteration or range queries - note it printed the keys in alphabetical order. Use `std::unordered_map` (hash table, O(1) average) for fast lookups when order doesn't matter. `std::list` rarely wins in practice. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```")
X("cpp", "cpp_stl", "medium", "How do I sort a vector of structs by multiple fields in C++?",
  '#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nstruct Emp { std::string name; int dept; int salary; };\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<Emp> e = {{"Ana", 2, 50}, {"Bo", 1, 70}, {"Cy", 2, 90}, {"Di", 1, 70}};\n    std::sort(e.begin(), e.end(), [](const Emp &a, const Emp &b) {\n        if (a.dept != b.dept) return a.dept < b.dept;\n        if (a.salary != b.salary) return a.salary > b.salary;\n        return a.name < b.name;\n    });\n    for (auto &x : e) std::cout << x.name << " ";\n    std::cout << "\\n";\n}',
  "Pass a lambda comparator to `std::sort`. It must define a strict weak ordering: return true when `a` should come before `b`, compare the fields in priority order, and fall through on ties. Here: department ascending, salary descending, then name. Output: `{out}`. Use `std::stable_sort` if equal elements must keep their original order.", "Bo Di Cy Ana")
X("cpp", "cpp_template", "medium", "Show a simple C++ function template and a class template.",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nT maxOf(T a, T b) { return a > b ? a : b; }\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nclass Box {\n    T value;\npublic:\n    explicit Box(T v) : value(v) {}\n    T get() const { return value; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << maxOf(3, 8) << " " << maxOf(2.5, 1.5) << " " << maxOf<std::string>("pear", "apple") << "\\n";\n    Box<int> b(42);\n    std::cout << b.get() << "\\n";\n}',
  "Templates let the compiler generate a version of the code per type at compile time: no runtime cost and full type safety. `T` is deduced from the arguments, or given explicitly as in `maxOf<std::string>`. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nThe type must support the operations used (`>` here); errors appear at instantiation and can be long, which C++20 concepts help to improve.")
X("cpp", "cpp_oop", "medium", "Why do I need virtual destructors in C++?",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <memory>\n\nstruct Base {\n    virtual ~Base() { std::cout << "~Base\\n"; }\n    virtual void speak() const { std::cout << "base\\n"; }\n};\nstruct Derived : Base {\n    ~Derived() override { std::cout << "~Derived\\n"; }\n    void speak() const override { std::cout << "derived\\n"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::unique_ptr<Base> p = std::make_unique<Derived>();\n    p->speak();\n}',
  "When you delete a derived object through a base pointer, the destructor must be `virtual` so the **derived** destructor runs too. Without it, deleting through `Base*` is undefined behaviour and derived resources leak. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nNote the destruction order: derived first, then base. `override` makes the compiler check that you are really overriding a virtual function.")
X("cpp", "cpp_stl", "medium", "What's a good way to remove elements from a vector in C++?",
  '#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8};\n    v.erase(std::remove_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }), v.end());\n    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << " ";\n    std::cout << "\\n" << v.size() << "\\n";\n}',
  "Use the **erase-remove idiom**: `remove_if` shifts the elements you keep to the front and returns the new logical end, and `erase` then deletes the leftover tail. Calling `remove_if` alone doesn't change the vector's size. Erasing inside a hand-written index loop is a classic bug source because indexes shift. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\n(C++20 adds `std::erase_if(v, pred)` for the same job.)")
X("cpp", "cpp_error", "hard", "Why does my C++ vector of pointers leak, and how do I fix it?",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <memory>\n#include <vector>\n\nstruct Item {\n    int id;\n    explicit Item(int i) : id(i) {}\n    ~Item() { std::cout << "free " << id << "\\n"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Item>> items;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) items.push_back(std::make_unique<Item>(i));\n    items.erase(items.begin());\n    std::cout << "size " << items.size() << "\\n";\n}',
  "A `std::vector<Item*>` frees only the pointers, not the objects they point to, so each `new` needs a manual `delete` and any early return or exception leaks. A `vector<unique_ptr<Item>>` owns its elements: erasing one destroys it immediately, and the rest are destroyed when the vector goes out of scope. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```")
X("cpp", "cpp_stl", "hard", "Priority queue with a custom ordering in C++?",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <queue>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nstruct Task { int pri; std::string name; };\nstruct Later { bool operator()(const Task &a, const Task &b) const { return a.pri > b.pri; } };\n\nint main() {\n    std::priority_queue<Task, std::vector<Task>, Later> q;\n    q.push({3, "write"}); q.push({1, "plan"}); q.push({2, "code"});\n    while (!q.empty()) { std::cout << q.top().name << " "; q.pop(); }\n    std::cout << "\\n";\n}',
  "`std::priority_queue` is a max-heap by default (largest on top). To get the smallest priority number first, supply a comparator that says \"a comes later than b\" when `a.pri > b.pri`; the container is a heap, so this inverts the order. Push and pop are O(log n). Output: `{out}`.", "plan code write")
X("cpp", "cpp_string", "easy", "How do I split a string by spaces in C++?",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string line = "the quick  brown fox";\n    std::istringstream in(line);\n    std::vector<std::string> words;\n    for (std::string w; in >> w;) words.push_back(w);\n    std::cout << words.size() << ": ";\n    for (auto &w : words) std::cout << "[" << w << "]";\n    std::cout << "\\n";\n}',
  "Wrap the string in an `istringstream` and extract with `>>`, which skips any amount of whitespace. That's why the double space produces no empty token. Output: `{out}`. For other delimiters use `std::getline(in, token, ',')`.", "4: [the][quick][brown][fox]")
X("cpp", "cpp_const", "medium", "What does const do on a method and a reference parameter?",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nclass Counter {\n    int n = 0;\npublic:\n    void inc() { n++; }\n    int value() const { return n; }   // promises not to modify *this\n};\n\nvoid show(const Counter &c) { std::cout << c.value() << "\\n"; }   // no copy, no modification\n\nint main() {\n    Counter c;\n    c.inc(); c.inc();\n    show(c);\n}',
  "A `const` member function promises not to change the object, so it can be called on `const` objects. Passing `const T&` avoids copying while preventing modification; inside `show` you can only call `const` methods. Output: `{out}`. Marking everything that can be `const` documents intent and lets the compiler catch mistakes.", "2")

# ================= Java =================
X("java", "java_collections", "medium", "ArrayList vs LinkedList vs HashMap in Java - how do I choose?",
  'import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(5, 3, 8));\n        list.add(1);\n        Map<String, Integer> ages = new HashMap<>();\n        ages.put("ana", 30);\n        ages.merge("ana", 1, Integer::sum);\n        ages.putIfAbsent("bo", 25);\n        Map<String, Integer> sorted = new TreeMap<>(ages);\n        System.out.println(list.get(2) + " " + sorted);\n    }\n}',
  "`ArrayList` is the default list: O(1) index access and amortised O(1) append. `LinkedList` rarely helps because pointer chasing is slow and index access is O(n). `HashMap` gives O(1) average key lookup with no order; `TreeMap` keeps keys sorted at O(log n). Handy methods: `merge` (combine or insert) and `putIfAbsent`. Output: `{out}`.")
X("java", "java_streams", "medium", "Give me a Java streams example that groups and counts.",
  'import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.stream.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<String> words = List.of("apple", "avocado", "banana", "blueberry", "cherry", "apricot");\n        Map<Character, Long> byLetter = words.stream()\n            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(w -> w.charAt(0), TreeMap::new, Collectors.counting()));\n        System.out.println(byLetter);\n        System.out.println(words.stream().filter(w -> w.length() > 6).map(String::toUpperCase).sorted().collect(Collectors.toList()));\n    }\n}',
  "Streams describe a pipeline: a source, intermediate steps (`filter`, `map`, `sorted`) and a terminal operation (`collect`). `groupingBy` with a downstream `counting()` builds a map of counts, and passing `TreeMap::new` keeps the keys ordered. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nStreams are lazy - nothing runs until the terminal operation. A plain loop is fine when it is clearer.")
X("java", "java_records", "medium", "What is a Java record and when should I use it?",
  'public class Main {\n    record Point(int x, int y) {\n        Point {\n            if (x < 0 || y < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative");\n        }\n        double dist() { return Math.sqrt(x * x + y * y); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Point p = new Point(3, 4);\n        System.out.println(p + " " + p.dist() + " " + p.equals(new Point(3, 4)));\n    }\n}',
  "A record is a concise, immutable data carrier: the compiler generates the constructor, accessors, `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString`. A compact constructor (the block without parameters) validates or normalises the fields. Output: `{out}`. Use records for value objects and DTOs. Don't use them for classes that need mutable state or inheritance.")
X("java", "java_interfaces", "medium", "Explain interfaces and polymorphism in Java with a short example.",
  'import java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    interface Shape { double area(); default String describe() { return getClass().getSimpleName() + " " + String.format("%.2f", area()); } }\n    record Circle(double r) implements Shape { public double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; } }\n    record Rect(double w, double h) implements Shape { public double area() { return w * h; } }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Shape> shapes = List.of(new Circle(1), new Rect(2, 3));\n        for (Shape s : shapes) System.out.println(s.describe());\n    }\n}',
  "An interface defines a contract; each implementation supplies its own behaviour, and code written against `Shape` works with any implementation without knowing the concrete type. That is polymorphism: the call to `area()` is resolved at runtime. `default` methods give shared behaviour. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```")
X("java", "java_exceptions", "medium", "How should I use try-with-resources and custom exceptions in Java?",
  'public class Main {\n    static class AppException extends Exception {\n        AppException(String msg, Throwable cause) { super(msg, cause); }\n    }\n\n    static class Res implements AutoCloseable {\n        final String n;\n        Res(String n) { this.n = n; System.out.println("open " + n); }\n        public void close() { System.out.println("close " + n); }\n    }\n\n    static void run() throws AppException {\n        try (Res a = new Res("a"); Res b = new Res("b")) {\n            Integer.parseInt("x");\n        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n            throw new AppException("bad input", e);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try { run(); } catch (AppException e) { System.out.println(e.getMessage() + " <- " + e.getCause().getClass().getSimpleName()); }\n    }\n}',
  "`try-with-resources` closes every `AutoCloseable` automatically, in reverse order, even when an exception is thrown. Wrap low-level exceptions in a meaningful exception of your own and keep the original as the *cause*, so the stack trace is preserved. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nCatch specific exceptions and avoid swallowing them silently.")
X("java", "java_generics", "hard", "How do bounded generics work in Java?",
  'import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static <T extends Comparable<T>> T maxOf(List<T> items) {\n        T best = items.get(0);\n        for (T x : items) if (x.compareTo(best) > 0) best = x;\n        return best;\n    }\n\n    static double sum(Collection<? extends Number> nums) {\n        double s = 0;\n        for (Number n : nums) s += n.doubleValue();\n        return s;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(maxOf(List.of(3, 9, 4)) + " " + maxOf(List.of("kiwi", "apple")));\n        System.out.println(sum(List.of(1, 2.5, 3L)));\n    }\n}',
  "`<T extends Comparable<T>>` restricts `T` to types that can be compared, so `compareTo` is available and the method works for integers, strings and your own comparable classes. `? extends Number` (a wildcard) accepts a collection of any `Number` subtype for reading. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nGenerics are erased at runtime, which is why you can't create `new T[]` or check `instanceof List<String>`.")
X("java", "java_equals", "medium", "Why don't my objects work as HashMap keys?",
  'import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static class Bad { final int id; Bad(int id) { this.id = id; } }\n    static class Good {\n        final int id; Good(int id) { this.id = id; }\n        @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Good g && g.id == id; }\n        @Override public int hashCode() { return Integer.hashCode(id); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<Bad, String> m1 = new HashMap<>();\n        m1.put(new Bad(1), "x");\n        Map<Good, String> m2 = new HashMap<>();\n        m2.put(new Good(1), "x");\n        System.out.println(m1.get(new Bad(1)) + " " + m2.get(new Good(1)));\n    }\n}',
  "`HashMap` finds a key using `hashCode()` and then `equals()`. Without overrides, both are based on object identity, so a new `Bad(1)` is a different key from the one you stored. Override **both**, consistently: equal objects must have equal hash codes. Output: `{out}`. Records, enums and `Objects.hash(...)` make this easy, and mutable fields used in `hashCode` make keys unsafe.")
X("java", "java_strings", "easy", "Why is string concatenation in a loop slow in Java?",
  'public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n            if (sb.length() > 0) sb.append(", ");\n            sb.append(i * i);\n        }\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        System.out.println(String.join("-", "a", "b", "c"));\n    }\n}',
  "`String` is immutable, so `s += x` in a loop creates a new string each time, copying everything: O(n^2) overall for n pieces. A `StringBuilder` appends into a growing buffer in amortised O(1) per piece. `String.join` is neater for separators. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```")
X("java", "java_concurrency", "hard", "How do I run tasks in parallel in Java and collect results?",
  'import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.concurrent.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);\n        List<Future<Integer>> fs = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int n = 1; n <= 4; n++) {\n            final int k = n;\n            fs.add(pool.submit(() -> k * k));\n        }\n        int total = 0;\n        for (Future<Integer> f : fs) total += f.get();\n        pool.shutdown();\n        System.out.println(total);\n    }\n}',
  "Use an `ExecutorService` rather than creating threads by hand: you submit `Callable`s, get `Future`s back, and `get()` blocks until each result is ready. Always `shutdown()` the pool or the JVM may not exit. The program sums 1+4+9+16 and prints `{out}`. For independent async steps, `CompletableFuture` composes better; for shared mutable state, prefer immutable data or `java.util.concurrent` classes over manual locking.", "30")
X("java", "java_optional", "medium", "How should I use Optional in Java without making it worse?",
  'import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static Optional<String> find(Map<Integer, String> db, int id) {\n        return Optional.ofNullable(db.get(id));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<Integer, String> db = Map.of(1, "ana");\n        System.out.println(find(db, 1).map(String::toUpperCase).orElse("unknown"));\n        System.out.println(find(db, 2).map(String::toUpperCase).orElse("unknown"));\n    }\n}',
  "`Optional` makes \"might be absent\" explicit in a return type, and `map`/`orElse` let you handle both cases without null checks. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nGuidelines: use it for return values, not for fields or method parameters; don't call `get()` without checking; and don't wrap collections (return an empty collection instead).")
X("java", "java_sorting", "medium", "Sort a list of objects by two fields in Java.",
  'import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    record Student(String name, int grade, double gpa) {}\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Student> s = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\n            new Student("Ana", 10, 3.5), new Student("Bo", 9, 3.9),\n            new Student("Cy", 10, 3.9), new Student("Di", 9, 3.9)));\n        s.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Student::grade)\n            .thenComparing(Student::gpa, Comparator.reverseOrder())\n            .thenComparing(Student::name));\n        s.forEach(x -> System.out.print(x.name() + " "));\n        System.out.println();\n    }\n}',
  "Build comparators with `Comparator.comparing...` and chain tie-breakers using `thenComparing`. Here: grade ascending, GPA descending, then name. Output: `{out}`. This is clearer and less error-prone than writing one big `compare` method with subtraction (which can overflow).", "Bo Di Cy Ana")
X("java", "java_basics", "easy", "What's the difference between == and equals() with Integer in Java?",
  'public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Integer a = 127, b = 127, c = 1000, d = 1000;\n        System.out.println((a == b) + " " + (c == d) + " " + c.equals(d));\n    }\n}',
  "For objects, `==` compares references. Autoboxing caches small `Integer` values (-128 to 127), so `a == b` happens to be true, while `c == d` creates two separate objects and is false. Output: `{out}`. Always compare boxed values with `equals()` (or unbox them first).", "true false true")

# ================= JavaScript / TypeScript =================
X("javascript", "js_event_loop", "hard", "Explain async/await error handling with a failing request.",
  'const fakeFetch = (url) => new Promise((resolve, reject) =>\n  setTimeout(() => (url.includes("bad") ? reject(new Error("404 " + url)) : resolve({ url, ok: true })), 5));\n\nasync function load(urls) {\n  const out = [];\n  for (const u of urls) {\n    try {\n      out.push((await fakeFetch(u)).url);\n    } catch (e) {\n      out.push("failed: " + e.message);\n    }\n  }\n  return out;\n}\n\nload(["/a", "/bad", "/c"]).then((r) => console.log(r));',
  "`await` pauses the async function until the promise settles; a rejection is thrown at the `await`, so ordinary `try/catch` works. Catching inside the loop lets one failure not abort the rest. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nThis loop is sequential. For independent requests, `Promise.allSettled` runs them in parallel.")
X("javascript", "js_data", "medium", "Group an array of objects by a property in JavaScript.",
  'const people = [\n  { name: "Ana", team: "red" }, { name: "Bo", team: "blue" },\n  { name: "Cy", team: "red" }, { name: "Di", team: "blue" }, { name: "Ed", team: "green" },\n];\nconst byTeam = people.reduce((acc, p) => {\n  (acc[p.team] ??= []).push(p.name);\n  return acc;\n}, {});\nconsole.log(byTeam);\nconsole.log(Object.entries(byTeam).map(([t, n]) => `${t}:${n.length}`).join(" "));',
  "`reduce` carries an accumulator object, and `??=` creates the array for a key the first time it is seen. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nNode 21+ also has `Object.groupBy(array, fn)` for exactly this; the reduce version works everywhere.")
X("javascript", "js_data", "medium", "How do I deep-clone an object safely in JavaScript?",
  'const original = { a: 1, nested: { list: [1, 2] }, when: new Date(0) };\nconst shallow = { ...original };\nconst deep = structuredClone(original);\noriginal.nested.list.push(3);\nconsole.log(shallow.nested.list.length, deep.nested.list.length, deep.when instanceof Date);',
  "The spread operator makes a **shallow** copy: nested objects are still shared, so the push shows through `shallow`. `structuredClone` copies deeply and handles dates, maps and sets. Output: `{out}`. `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))` also works for plain data but turns dates into strings and drops functions and `undefined`.", "3 2 true")
X("javascript", "js_perf", "medium", "Array.includes in a loop vs a Set - show me it's worth changing.",
  'const big = Array.from({ length: 5000 }, (_, i) => i * 2);\nconst probe = Array.from({ length: 2000 }, (_, i) => i);\nlet viaArray = 0;\nfor (const p of probe) if (big.includes(p)) viaArray++;\nconst set = new Set(big);\nlet viaSet = 0;\nfor (const p of probe) if (set.has(p)) viaSet++;\nconsole.log(viaArray, viaSet, viaArray === viaSet);',
  "`includes` scans the array (O(n)) for each probe, so the first loop is O(n x m). A `Set` lookup is O(1) on average, so building it once and querying makes it O(n + m). Both loops count the same matches: `{out}`. The gain grows with input size.")
X("javascript", "js_dom_free", "medium", "How does event delegation work? Can you show the idea without a browser?",
  'class Emitter {\n  constructor() { this.handlers = []; }\n  on(fn) { this.handlers.push(fn); }\n  emit(target) { this.handlers.forEach((h) => h(target)); }\n}\n\nconst list = new Emitter();\nlist.on((t) => { if (t.tag === "li") console.log("clicked", t.text); });\n[{ tag: "li", text: "one" }, { tag: "span", text: "x" }, { tag: "li", text: "two" }].forEach((t) => list.emit(t));',
  "Event delegation means attaching **one** listener to a parent and checking which child triggered the event, instead of attaching a listener to every child. In the browser, `event.target` (or `event.target.closest(\"li\")`) plays the role of `t`. It uses less memory and automatically covers items added later. Output of this simulation:\n\n```\n{out}\n```")
X("typescript", "ts_types", "hard", "How do I make a typed result type in TypeScript instead of throwing?",
  'type Result<T, E> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E };\n\nfunction parsePort(text: string): Result<number, string> {\n  const n = Number(text);\n  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 1 || n > 65535) return { ok: false, error: `invalid port: ${text}` };\n  return { ok: true, value: n };\n}\n\nfor (const t of ["8080", "99999", "abc"]) {\n  const r = parsePort(t);\n  console.log(r.ok ? `port ${r.value}` : r.error);\n}',
  "A discriminated union makes the failure case part of the function's type: callers cannot read `value` without first checking `ok`, because TypeScript narrows the type inside each branch. This suits expected errors such as validation, while exceptions suit unexpected ones. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```")
X("typescript", "ts_types", "medium", "What are utility types like Partial and Pick used for?",
  'interface User { id: number; name: string; email: string }\n\nfunction update(user: User, changes: Partial<Pick<User, "name" | "email">>): User {\n  return { ...user, ...changes };\n}\n\nconst u: User = { id: 1, name: "Ana", email: "a@x.io" };\nconsole.log(update(u, { name: "Anna" }));',
  "`Pick<User, \"name\" | \"email\">` selects just those fields, and `Partial<...>` makes them optional, so the function accepts a patch that cannot touch `id`. Passing `{ id: 5 }` would be a compile-time error. Output (types vanish at runtime):\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nOther common utilities are `Omit`, `Required`, `Readonly` and `Record`.")
X("typescript", "ts_types", "hard", "Show me a generic function with a constraint in TypeScript.",
  'function groupBy<T, K extends string | number>(items: T[], key: (item: T) => K): Record<K, T[]> {\n  const out = {} as Record<K, T[]>;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const k = key(item);\n    (out[k] ??= []).push(item);\n  }\n  return out;\n}\n\nconsole.log(groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], (n) => (n % 2 === 0 ? "even" : "odd")));',
  "`T` is the item type, and `K extends string | number` constrains the key to something usable as an object key. TypeScript infers both from the call, so the result is typed as `Record<\"even\" | \"odd\", number[]>`. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```")
